# Working with your desktop

wm remembers which workspaces your windows belong to and arranges them for you. It also
checks what applications actually did after asking them to move, resize, or take focus.
Sometimes an application responds slowly, limits its window size, or temporarily stops
reporting its windows. Here's what to expect in those situations.

## Windows keep their workspace through interruptions

If your Mac sleeps or an application temporarily stops responding, wm may lose sight of
some windows. That does not mean you closed them. wm remembers their workspace
assignments and avoids trying to arrange windows it cannot currently read.

When wm can see the same windows again, they return to their assigned workspaces. There
is no time limit on that remembered placement while wm is running. A long sleep should
not make a returning window join whichever workspace happens to be active.

For example, if you leave OpenSCAD on workspace 3 and put your Mac to sleep while viewing
workspace C, the same OpenSCAD window should still belong to workspace 3 after wake.
An application that takes longer to respond may become manageable later than the rest
of the desktop.

## New windows follow your placement rules

A returning window keeps its previous assignment, including a workspace you chose
manually. A genuinely new window follows your [configured assignment rules](/wm/docs/configuration/).
If no rule matches, wm places it in your current workspace.

If you focus a new window that is assigned to another workspace, wm reveals that
workspace. Simply discovering a window in the background does not switch workspaces.

Closing a window and opening a new one starts a new assignment. Reopening the same
document does not necessarily restore the old workspace: matching titles alone are not
enough to identify a returning window.

Use [window assignment](/wm/docs/commands/windows/assign/) to choose a workspace yourself.

## Applications can limit their window sizes

Some applications will not resize below a minimum size, will not grow beyond a maximum,
or use fixed-size windows. Others animate toward a requested size rather than changing
immediately.

wm checks the resulting size instead of assuming the application accepted its request.
It may try another supported way to arrange the window or adjust the layout to a size
the application allows. When wm establishes a reliable size limit, it can use that
information in later layouts.

This means a layout may take a moment to settle or differ from an evenly divided grid.
wm cannot force an application to support a size it refuses.

## Focus follows the window you're using

When you select a window outside wm—for example, by switching applications—wm follows
that window to its workspace. An inactive application cannot select your workspace just
because it still considers one of its own windows focused.

When a command asks to focus a window, wm checks whether that window actually received
focus. Bringing an application forward is not always enough: the application may select
a different window, or the requested window may no longer be available.

## A change can fail without losing your assignments

Moving several windows requires several requests to applications. An application can
stop responding partway through a workspace change, so wm cannot make every desktop
change happen at once.

wm makes a bounded number of attempts rather than retrying indefinitely. If a compound
command fails, wm tries to undo moves it already made. That recovery can also fail if an
application is still unresponsive, so the visible desktop may need another check before
it matches your intended layout.

A failed command means wm could not complete and verify the requested result. It does
not, by itself, mean that your windows were closed or that their workspace assignments
should be forgotten.

## When you want to arrange things yourself

You can [pause wm](/wm/docs/commands/system/pause/) to stop automatic window management
while you make manual changes. [Resume](/wm/docs/commands/system/resume/) when you're
ready for wm to check the desktop and apply its layout again.

If an application is slow to recover after wake, let it become responsive before
retrying a failed command. You can also [request a fresh layout check](/wm/docs/commands/system/reconcile/).
That gives wm another chance to read the current windows; it cannot make an unresponsive
application respond.
